[ews00461@uxa.cso.uiuc.edu writes:]
[Subject: Peaceful monsters ?]
[] When I attack something and Nethack asks me for confirmation, does that
[] mean I attacked a friend ?  The comments here about confirmation for
[] attacking peaceful monsters seem to say that...

well, it's complicated and i'm glad you asked ...

the confirmation is a method used to warn you of an action which may not
be in your best interest. specifically, the attack on co-aligned monsters
(hence peaceful) is bad for your alignment. killing them is even worse!
unfortunately, there is currently no intuitive or common sense way to
know this easily by simply playing the game.

co-alignment often implies peacefulness but the opposite isn't necessarily
true, i.e. shopkeepers don't turn anyone away without a good reason,
which has nothing to do with alignment.

the way to exploit the current usage of confirmations in nethack is to
decline at every oppurtunity. that way you'll be safe. there may be an
exception but you get the point (hopefully). how is that strategic? don't
ask me, i dunno ...

do confirmations belong in nethack? in my personal opinion, no, no way,
no way jose', nada, unh unh, negative, negatory, etc.. ideally (to me)
the game should _always_ interpret your actions as being purposeful.
a knowledgable player would _intentionally_ avoid pitfalls or cause
certain things to happen. an inexperienced player would make mistakes
and using common sense should potentially be able to undo the wrong that
was done. this is admittedly ideal. i make no allowances for "line noise"
or "slow response" since i wouldn't use a word processor (for example)
under those conditions unless i had to and then i wouldn't complain.
to me, the need/usage of confirmations is an admission of guilt in
the sense that the interface is somehow wrong. some examples:

a blessed potion of invisibility should unconditionally make you
intrinsically invisible, no questions asked. you should be able to
control your invisibility by a ring of invisibility (control). if
you're currently visible then wearing the ring makes you invisible. if
you're currently invisible then wearing the ring makes you visible.
if your invisibility times-out while wearing the ring you should notice
the change (oppositely) and get a good message to that effect. this isn't
trivial since several checks are needed but it would be ideal, to me.
this was a simple example (and perhaps not completely appropriate).

attacking peaceful monsters should be something you could do at your
discretion if somehow you could discriminate peaceful monsters from
other nasties, no questions asked. again, the premise here is that a
reasonably well-informed player would deliberately avoid jeopardizing
her/his alignment unless the circumstances warranted otherwise. to me,
confirmation doesn't make the player "wiser" it just obscures the
problem. how do you code this (i.e. make the player wiser)??? hmmm,
i dunno (confirmation is clearly easier but it ain't right). perhaps
a reward for "strident alignment" would be "intrinsic alignment
protection" which would cause you to miss (once) an attempt at hitting
a peaceful monster with the message: "you narrowly miss hitting the
_peaceful_ dwarf", as an example. this wouldn't anger the dwarf who
presumably wouldn't notice the act. the "narrow miss" should not cost
you a move and should wear off after so many moves so you could narrowly
miss again in the future in the event that you've left the level or
forgotten the monster, etc.  if you persist in attacking/killing peaceful
monsters then you'd become proficient at it (bad alignment) and you'd no
longer narrowly miss.  perhaps peaceful monsters could be identified
visually (i dunno how).  _if_ a player could discriminate peaceful then his
actions could be interpreted as _purposeful_. significant actions should
_always_ be accompanied by an appropriate feeling/message (i.e. you ate the
little dog, you hear a bolt of lightning in the distance, and you'd have to
make the association to the fact that from that point on monsters seem to be
aggravated around you.

cruising aimlessly around a treacherous dungeon and falling into a
potentially drowning pool of water should be a problem! you shouldn't
be able to prevent this by saying "i never want to fall into pools
and so if i attempt to then please warn me and give me an oppurtunity
to not make a mistake that i'll regret". the strategy involved is that
you must, at this point, be on your toes (i.e. pay attention, take care
and be deliberate). defeating the threat with a "I-really-know-better"
confirmation option should not be considered equal to defeating the
threat by superior play (i.e. that's a different game). the typo arguments
(carelessness, inattentiveness or "line noise", etc.) don't hold sway
with me since the game should interpret your actions as being purposeful,
no second guessing. on the other hand, falling into a pool shouldn't be
certain death either. this clearly implies that the interface requires
some deliberation in this regard (and the need/usage of the confirmation
method underscores the problem but doesn't solve it, in my opinion).
a "wise" player should, on occasion, have a good reason to jump into a
pool and would properly prepare. that preparation would also alleviate the
problem of accidently falling into a pool. a fool might fall into a pool
and would hopefully learn by the experience, no confirmation required.

if you eat when you're satiated (especially after you've been warned
that you're having a hard time getting all this food down) then you
should run the risk of choking, no questions asked. of course, other
things should be possible, as well ...

confirmations belong in explore-mode where it is more appropriate to
second guess a player's actions. so do a few other "options" such as
not HARD. the real game should be essentially the same for all players
(this has other implications, also). to play or not to play is your
decision and when you decide to play then there should no questions
asked ... so to speak ... (you should, of course, complain
_constructively_ about things you don't like).

oh yeah, the game should ask about things that are not strategic like
"really quit?", "really save?", "really shell escape?" etc.

these are my thoughts on confirmation. i'd like to hear arguments about
the merits or lack of merits in these positions and other positions
on the matter. reasonable arguments preferred. e-mail is okay. it almost
goes without saying that the messenger isn't the issue. 

